  "overview": "Unlike every board in the Microcontrollers category, the Raspberry Pi is a genuine computer: it boots a full Linux operating system from a microSD card, runs a desktop environment, browses the web, and can be SSH'd into like any server. That changes the robotics use case entirely — instead of writing firmware that runs one program forever, you write regular Python/C++ programs on top of an OS with real multitasking, a filesystem, and networking. The tradeoff is that it needs a proper shutdown command before losing power (yanking power can corrupt the SD card) and its 40-pin GPIO header is 3.3V-only with no built-in protection — frying a GPIO pin with 5V is a common and often fatal mistake since, unlike a $2 microcontroller, the whole board is the cost of the mistake.",
  "simpleSummary": "A full tiny computer that runs Linux, unlike the simpler single-task Arduino boards.",
